# Line endings for the transport repo.
#
# This file ships as `gitattributes`, without the dot, for the same reason the
# ignore file does: it has to govern the transport repo rather than the skill
# repo carrying it. bootstrap.sh restores the dot on the way out. Do not "fix"
# the name.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, stated accurately, because the obvious version of the story
# is wrong. Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true at install time, so a
# transport repo cloned on Windows arrives with CRLF throughout. That on its
# own is harmless: Git for Windows' bash strips CR transparently and behaves
# exactly as it does on LF, pipefail and set -u included. Measured on Windows
# Server 2022 with Git 2.55, both executed and sourced.
#
# The damage is to the OTHER machines, and it is what makes this a
# cross-platform repo problem rather than a Windows one. If CRLF ever gets
# COMMITTED - a contributor with autocrlf=false, a file written by a Windows
# editor, a step that generates one - then every Linux clone of this repo gets
# it too, and Linux bash does not forgive it:
#
#   Executed, it stops with a clear complaint:
#       /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
#   Sourced, which is how caplib.sh is loaded, it does NOT stop:
#       caplib.sh: line 2: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#   The CR attaches to `pipefail`, so the whole `set -uo pipefail` call fails
#   and NEITHER option is applied. The loop then runs with unset-variable
#   checking and pipefail both silently off, on the machine you cannot log in
#   to, and the first thing you learn about it is a log that is wrong rather
#   than absent.
#
# So this file is not protecting Windows from itself. It is what lets a Windows
# operator and a Linux container share one transport repo: the content stays LF
# whatever anyone's core.autocrlf happens to be, and that setting belongs to
# whoever cloned rather than to us.

# text=auto still lets git detect and leave binaries alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the files whose breakage is silent, so that grepping this
# file for the extension that just failed you finds an answer.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Captured logs are the product of this repo and are read on every platform.
*.txt  text eol=lf
*.log  text eol=lf
